CARDS = [
# ---------- Node 30 ----------
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "What is a signal and how is it delivered?", "A software interrupt: a small integer with no payload. The kernel sets a bit in the target's pending mask; when that process is next about to return to user mode, the kernel applies the default action or arranges the stack so it resumes inside the handler. So a signal is delivered when the process next runs, not when it is sent."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "Give the numbers and meanings of the eight signals that matter.", "1 <code>SIGHUP</code> (terminal gone; reused as reload); 2 <code>SIGINT</code> (Ctrl-C); 9 <code>SIGKILL</code> (uncatchable); 11 <code>SIGSEGV</code>; 13 <code>SIGPIPE</code>; 15 <code>SIGTERM</code> (the polite request, <code>kill</code>'s default); 17 <code>SIGCHLD</code>; 19/18 <code>SIGSTOP</code>/<code>SIGCONT</code>."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "Why is there no such thing as cleanup on SIGKILL?", "It cannot be caught, blocked or ignored — deliberately, so there is always a way to stop a process that refuses to cooperate. No flush, no close, no final log line. Which is why every graceful shutdown design must live on <code>SIGTERM</code>, and why an OOM kill gives you nothing."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "Why may a signal handler not call <code>malloc</code>?", "It runs at an arbitrary point, possibly inside <code>malloc</code> holding the allocator's lock, so calling it deadlocks against the interrupted code. POSIX defines a short list of async-signal-safe functions; <code>printf</code> and <code>malloc</code> are not on it."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "What is the self-pipe trick, and why is your Node <code>SIGTERM</code> handler unrestricted?", "The handler writes one byte to a pipe and returns; the main loop reads it as an ordinary event. Linux later added <code>signalfd</code>. libuv does exactly this, so by the time your JavaScript callback runs it is a normal event-loop callback, not a signal handler."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "Why must a <code>SIGCHLD</code> handler reap in a loop?", "Standard signals do not queue — the pending set is a bitmask, so ten <code>SIGCHLD</code>s while one is blocked leave one bit. <code>while (waitpid(-1, &amp;st, WNOHANG) &gt; 0);</code>."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "What are PID 1's two special kernel behaviours?", "Signals with default actions are not delivered to it — the kernel refuses, so an unhandled <code>SIGTERM</code> is silently discarded. And it inherits orphans and is expected to reap them."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "Why does your container appear to take ten seconds to stop?", "<code>docker stop</code> sends <code>SIGTERM</code>, waits ten seconds, then <code>SIGKILL</code>s. Node as PID 1 with no handler discards the first signal entirely. It is not slowness — the polite request was thrown away."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "Why does <code>CMD npm start</code> break shutdown?", "It runs <code>sh -c \"npm start\"</code>, so a shell is PID 1 and does not forward signals to its child. Your Node process never hears about the shutdown. <code>ENTRYPOINT [\"node\", \"server.js\"]</code> — exec form — fixes it, and this is the most common Dockerfile defect in the wild."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "Why is the ordering in a Kubernetes graceful shutdown a race, and what is the fix?", "When a pod is deleted, <code>SIGTERM</code> and endpoint removal happen <em>concurrently</em>, and the removal takes time to propagate through kube-proxy on every node. Stopping accept immediately refuses traffic still being routed to you — a burst of 502s on every deploy. Delay the actual shutdown a few seconds, or use a <code>preStop</code> sleep."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "Write the shape of a correct Node graceful shutdown.", "Fail readiness first and wait for the load balancer's probe period; then <code>server.close()</code> and <code>closeIdleConnections()</code>; then drain workers and the database pool; and set a hard <code>process.exit</code> deadline below <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> so you always exit on your own terms rather than being <code>SIGKILL</code>ed."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "Why does Node give you <code>EPIPE</code> rather than <code>SIGPIPE</code>?", "Node ignores <code>SIGPIPE</code> and surfaces the condition as a stream error, which is why writing to a socket the client already closed emits <code>'error'</code> — and why an unhandled <code>'error'</code> listener crashes the process. A client navigating away mid-response is normal traffic, not a fault."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "What is <code>SIGUSR1</code> used for in Node, and why is that useful in production?", "It opens the debugger port, which is a genuinely useful way to attach a profiler to a running production process without restarting it."),
("node-30", "Node 30 · Signals", "Why is reaching for <code>kill -9</code> first a habit worth losing?", "It skips every cleanup path the program has. If <code>SIGTERM</code> did not work the reason is worth knowing — usually a missing handler, a shell as PID 1, or a <code>D</code>-state hang that <code>-9</code> will not fix either."),
# ---------- Node 31 ----------
("node-31", "Node 31 · IPC", "What three things does every IPC mechanism trade?", "Speed, whether the parties must be related, and whether it works across machines."),
("node-31", "Node 31 · IPC", "What can a Unix domain socket do that a TCP socket cannot?", "Pass file descriptors (<code>SCM_RIGHTS</code>) — transferring an open descriptor to another process, where it becomes a new number referring to the same open file description — and report verified peer credentials (<code>SO_PEERCRED</code>): the connecting process's real UID, GID and PID, checked by the kernel and unforgeable."),
("node-31", "Node 31 · IPC", "Why is shared memory the fastest and also the most dangerous?", "The same physical frames appear in two address spaces, so there is no copy at all — and no synchronisation whatsoever, so you must supply your own atomics or locks, and a bug is a data race across a process boundary."),
("node-31", "Node 31 · IPC", "Give the default choice for local IPC and justify it.", "Unix domain sockets: as fast as anything that copies, the same API as networking so code ports trivially, and they carry descriptors and credentials. Reach for shared memory only when profiling proves the copy is the bottleneck."),
("node-31", "Node 31 · IPC", "What does <code>child.send(obj)</code> cost, and what is it wrong for?", "Serialisation plus two copies plus two syscalls over a Unix socket pair — cheap for coordination, completely wrong for a shared cache. The listening socket, by contrast, is passed once as a descriptor via <code>SCM_RIGHTS</code>."),
("node-31", "Node 31 · IPC", "Why is <code>worker_threads</code> messaging not IPC?", "Workers are threads in one process, so <code>postMessage</code> is an in-memory structured clone with no kernel involvement — no syscall, no socket. <code>transferList</code> avoids even the copy, and <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> is shared memory with <code>Atomics</code> as the synchronisation."),
("node-31", "Node 31 · IPC", "What do you gain by pointing nginx at a Unix socket instead of loopback TCP?", "No handshake, no ephemeral port allocation, no <code>TIME_WAIT</code> accumulation, and lower latency — plus the service becomes unreachable from the network by construction. The same applies to Postgres, whose <code>local</code> lines in <code>pg_hba.conf</code> use <code>peer</code> authentication via <code>SO_PEERCRED</code>."),
("node-31", "Node 31 · IPC", "Why is mounting <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> into a container a host compromise?", "The daemon authorises by peer credentials, so anything that can talk to it can start a privileged container, mount the host filesystem, and read every secret on the machine. Alternatives: a rootless daemon, a socket proxy whitelisting specific API calls, or a daemonless builder like Kaniko or rootless BuildKit."),
("node-31", "Node 31 · IPC", "Explain Redis's architectural role as a consequence of Node 5.", "Separate processes have separate address spaces, so once you have cluster workers or replicas across machines, nothing local works between them. Rate limiter counters, session stores, cache, pub/sub for websocket fan-out and BullMQ queues are one problem — state visible to processes with separate address spaces — solved by a process everyone can reach over a socket."),
# ---------- Node 32 ----------
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Why does the socket API use file descriptors?", "4.2BSD, 1983, Berkeley: reusing the descriptor meant network endpoints could be read and written like anything else — Node 21's uniform interface paying off again."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "What identifies a TCP connection, and why does that matter?", "The four-tuple: source address, source port, destination address, destination port. The kernel hashes it to route an incoming packet to the right socket, and it is what bounds ephemeral port exhaustion."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Name the two backlog queues and what each fills from.", "The SYN queue holds half-open connections awaiting the final ACK (<code>tcp_max_syn_backlog</code>; SYN floods target it, and SYN cookies defend by encoding state into the sequence number). The accept queue holds established connections waiting for your program to call <code>accept()</code> (the <code>listen()</code> backlog, capped by <code>somaxconn</code>) — it fills when your process is not accepting fast enough."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "How do you detect a blocked event loop from the network side?", "<code>ss -lnt</code> shows <code>Recv-Q</code> as the current accept-queue depth against <code>Send-Q</code> as its limit, and <code>nstat -az TcpExtListenOverflows</code> counts drops. Rising overflows with healthy CPU is one of the cleanest possible diagnoses."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Distinguish flow control from congestion control.", "Flow control protects the <em>receiver</em>: every ACK carries a receive window and the sender may not exceed it — TCP-level backpressure. Congestion control protects the <em>network</em>: the sender grows a congestion window on success and shrinks it on loss."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Why does the first ~14 KB of a response arrive faster than the rest?", "Slow start begins with about ten packets and doubles each round trip, so a larger response takes several round trips to ramp. That is where the advice about keeping critical CSS under 14 KB came from."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "What does BBR do differently from CUBIC?", "CUBIC treats loss as the congestion signal — grow, halve on loss. BBR models the path's bandwidth and round-trip time directly, so it performs far better on links that drop packets for reasons other than congestion: mobile and long-distance. Enabling it on an edge server sometimes produces a startling improvement."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "What is bandwidth-delay product and when does it cap you?", "A 100 ms round trip at 1 Gbps needs about 12 MB in flight to keep the pipe full; if the receive buffer is smaller, throughput is capped by latency regardless of available bandwidth. Hence buffer autotuning and slow long-distance transfers."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Give the two real reasons for <code>TIME_WAIT</code>.", "A delayed duplicate packet from this connection must not be delivered to a new connection reusing the same four-tuple; and if the final ACK is lost, the peer retransmits its FIN and someone must answer. It lasts 60 seconds on Linux and is not tunable without recompiling."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Why does a client opening ~500 connections per second to one destination start failing?", "The side that closes first accumulates <code>TIME_WAIT</code> sockets for a minute each, and the ephemeral port range is about 28,000 ports — so it exhausts them and gets <code>EADDRNOTAVAIL</code>. The real fix is keep-alive and pooling, not tuning."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Distinguish <code>SO_REUSEADDR</code> from <code>SO_REUSEPORT</code>.", "<code>SO_REUSEADDR</code> lets a server bind a port that has connections in <code>TIME_WAIT</code> — without it, restarting fails with <code>EADDRINUSE</code> for a minute, which is why every framework sets it. <code>SO_REUSEPORT</code> (Linux 3.9, 2013) lets several processes bind the <em>same</em> port with the kernel load-balancing between them, which is what nginx workers use."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Decode <code>ECONNREFUSED</code>, <code>ETIMEDOUT</code>, <code>ECONNRESET</code> and <code>EPIPE</code>.", "Refused: the destination sent RST — reachable, nothing listening; fast and definitive. Timed out on connect: no reply at all — a firewall dropping silently or a wrong address. Reset: the peer sent RST on an established connection — it crashed, closed with unread data, a load balancer idle timeout, or a keep-alive socket reused as the server closed it. EPIPE: you wrote to a connection the peer has closed."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Why does <code>ECONNRESET</code> appear intermittently on a pooled HTTP client, and what fixes it?", "A race: your agent picks a pooled socket the server is simultaneously closing. Keep the client's idle timeout below the server's, and retry idempotent requests on it."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Why is <code>keepAlive: true</code> the single highest-value line in a Lambda calling other services?", "A new HTTPS connection costs a TCP round trip, a TLS round trip and slow start; reuse costs nothing. Node's <code>http.Agent</code> defaulted to <code>keepAlive: false</code> until Node 19, and a function instance handles many invocations."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "What is a Kubernetes Service, mechanically, and what does that break?", "Packet rewriting, not a proxy: kube-proxy programs netfilter (iptables, IPVS, or eBPF) so a packet to the Service IP has its destination rewritten to a chosen pod IP, on the sending node. Load balancing is per <em>connection</em>, so a long-lived HTTP/2 or gRPC connection pins to one pod forever — which is why gRPC clients and meshes need explicit balancing."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "What causes intermittent connection failures across every workload on one node with all applications healthy?", "The <code>nf_conntrack</code> table is full — NAT and stateful firewalling require remembering every connection, and when the table fills new connections are dropped with a kernel log line. Check <code>conntrack -C</code> against <code>nf_conntrack_max</code>."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Explain head-of-line blocking at three layers and how HTTP evolved past each.", "HTTP/1.1 blocks at the request layer (hence six connections per host). HTTP/2 multiplexes streams over one connection, removing it at the HTTP layer — but not at TCP's, where one lost packet stalls every stream. HTTP/3 abandons TCP for QUIC over UDP, doing reliability per stream in user space, and merges the transport and TLS handshakes."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "What is a PMTU black hole and what is its signature?", "Overlay networks and VPNs add encapsulation, lowering the effective MTU; when a firewall blocks the ICMP fragmentation-needed message, path MTU discovery fails silently. Signature: small requests succeed and large POST bodies hang forever on the same connection path."),
("node-32", "Node 32 · The network stack", "Why is DNS slow inside a Kubernetes pod, and what are the fixes?", "The default <code>ndots: 5</code> means any name with fewer than five dots is tried against every search domain first — four or five failing queries, for both A and AAAA. Fixes: a trailing dot, an explicit <code>dnsConfig</code> with lower <code>ndots</code>, or NodeLocal DNSCache. And <code>dns.lookup</code> uses the threadpool, so slow DNS also blocks file I/O."),
]
